{"request":{"path":"/rest/contents/search","query":{"limit":12,"offset":0,"projection":"default","sort":"publishedAt:desc"},"body":{"operator":"and","filters":{"format":{"includes":["video"]},"profileId":{"includes":["prf_d4f46bb2d6ee59c46a3daef880f2"]},"publishedAt":{"after":"2026-03-27","before":"2026-09-27"}}}},"status":200,"fetchedAt":"2026-09-27T10:31:24.399Z","response":{"data":{"aggregations":{"totalViewsCount":542508,"totalInteractionsCount":9757},"results":[{"id":"cnt_b6516257e86253278a285d0f07fd","matchedQueries":[],"platform":"instagram","profileHandle":"drmrinalibhatia","profileDisplayName":"Dr. Mrinaali | Content Creator","format":"video","caption":"Daayra is technically excellent and morally evasive and I'm not sure those two things can coexist for 143 minutes without the film eventually collapsing under the weight of its own contradiction. Prithviraj is exceptional. Kareena is doing something genuinely subtle. The first hour crackles. The climax blinks. But the most interesting thing about it isn't the encounter debate at all. It's the gap. The gap between when a crime is reported and when anything is actually done. That's the film at its most honest and most angry and it doesn't stay there long enough. \n\nWhat I find fascinating about Daayra technically is how much work the edit is doing that the screenplay doesn't. Charu Shree Roy is one of the best editors working in Hindi cinema right now Killa, Chhichhore, Sitaare Zameen Par and here she and Gulzar are cutting a film that lives and breathes in the space between scenes, not inside them. The non-linear structure is actually the film's most defensible argument. When the writing softens in the second half and the pacing falters, you feel the edit losing its grip. That's when Daayra starts to slide.\n\nDaayra is a technically well-made, often absorbing procedural that asks an important question and then flinches from it. The first hour is strong. Prithviraj is exceptional. The editing is genuinely inventive. But the second half softens, the climax underdelivers, and the film's claimed moral neutrality is undercut by the fact that only one of its two lead characters gets to feel human.\n\nWorth watching especially for Prithviraj's performance and for the subject matter, which demands engagement. But it's a lesser Meghna Gulzar film. Talvar remains the high-water mark. And unlike Talvar, Daayra won't haunt you as much as it should.\n\nWho should watch it: Anyone interested in the Hyderabad case, the ethics of police encounters, or how Hindi cinema handles real-world crime. Procedural fans. Prithviraj fans.\n\nWho might struggle: Viewers wanting plot twists or a clear moral resolution. Viewers hoping Kareena gets the material she deserves.\n\n#Daayra #FilmEditing #MeghnaGulzar #CharuShreeRoy #FilmCraft BollywoodAnalysis @svetlanasharma @marudharentertainmentnetwork","captionLanguage":"en","thumbnailMediaId":"med_85f14af4459bbba1268d2758847f","publishedAt":"2026-09-19T10:14:37.000Z","profileId":"prf_d4f46bb2d6ee59c46a3daef880f2","thumbnailMediaUrl":"https://oriane-contents.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/instagram/DddRxkXoZ56/thumbnail.webp","viewsCount":18488,"likesCount":230,"sharesCount":400,"commentsCount":50,"interactionsCount":680,"engagementRatePerViews":3.678061445261791,"engagementRatePerFollowers":1.3018589780407022,"profileFollowersCount":52233,"profileFollowingCount":7839,"profilePostsCount":783,"mediaCount":1,"duration":65.734,"hashtags":["#daayra","#filmediting","#meghnagulzar","#charushreeroy","#filmcraft"],"coAuthors":[{"id":"prf_abe7b5349cb53e152aa253ce4da0","platformId":"21943591580","profileHandle":"karaksocial"}],"mentions":[{"id":null,"platformId":null,"profileHandle":"svetlanasharma"},{"id":null,"platformId":null,"profileHandle":"marudharentertainmentnetwork"},{"id":null,"platformId":null,"profileHandle":"desitalentnetwork"}]},{"id":"cnt_3dd67c50e5ba51689e1b0b7f8655","matchedQueries":[],"platform":"instagram","profileHandle":"drmrinalibhatia","prof